 ## Tiered Compilation
 
-Shape uses a three-tier compilation strategy. Every function starts interpreted
-and is promoted to higher tiers based on observed call frequency.
+:::caution[Not wired into execution today]
+Everything in this section is implemented and unit-tested, but nothing reaches
+it at runtime. `VirtualMachine`'s tier manager is never installed outside
+tests, so no call counter is consulted, no compilation request is ever sent to
+the background thread, and no interpreter call path looks up native code.
+
+What `--mode jit` actually does is compile the whole program ahead of time,
+once, before execution — the pipeline described under
+[Scoped Per-Function JIT](#scoped-per-function-jit) and
+[`--mode jit` semantics](#--mode-jit-semantics). A function does not get faster
+after 100 calls; it is either native from the start or interpreted throughout.
+
+Read this section as the design of the tiering pipeline, not as a description
+of what runs. Making it the default execution mode is tracked in
+[#187](https://github.com/shape-lang/shape/issues/187).
+:::
+
+Shape's tiered design uses a three-tier strategy. Every function starts
+interpreted and is promoted to higher tiers based on observed call frequency.
 
