+### Per-function fallback
+
+Some constructs have a known divergence between what the JIT would emit and
+what the interpreter does. The compiler records each one against the function
+that contains it, and that function is left as an `Interpreted` entry in the
+mixed function table. Calls into it route through the fallback trampoline; the
+rest of the program keeps its native code.
+
+```shape runnable
+fn halve(n: int) -> Result<int, string> {
+    if n % 2 != 0 {
+        return Err("odd")
+    }
+    return Ok(n / 2)
+}
+
+fn quarter(n: int) -> Result<int, string> {
+    let h = halve(n)?      // `?` — only this function runs interpreted
+    return halve(h)
+}
+
+fn scale(x: number, factor: number) -> number {
+    return x * factor      // unaffected: still compiled natively
+}
+
+print(quarter(20))
+print(scale(3.0, 4.0))
+```
+
+```
+Ok(5)
+12.0
+```
+
+Three constructs are handled this way today: the `?` operator, the `??`
+null-coalescing operator, and an imported `pub const` read at its use site.
+
+Two constructs still cost the whole program its native execution, because the
+divergence spans a producer and a consumer that the per-function record covers
+only one half of:
+
+| Construct | Why it is not narrowed to one function |
+|---|---|
+| A direct call to an imported standard-library function | The callee is resolved through a hidden module binding. A native toplevel plus an interpreted function reading that binding would see different values for it. |
+| A reference returned by a function declared `-> &T` | The record is attached to the code that *reads* the reference, but the reference-counted cell it points into is created by the function that *returns* it. Interpreting only the reader would leave a native producer handing back a raw stack address. |
+
+A construct in toplevel code — outside any function — also costs the whole
+program, because toplevel is what the JIT compiles as the entry point and there
+is no smaller unit to demote.
+
+The set of whole-program refusals is inventoried and may only shrink; the
+target is that none remain.
+
